[{:db/id -1
  :readme/name "macos-inventory"
  :readme/org "kotoba-lang"
  :readme/role "reusable library — read-only macOS system inventory (persistence, privacy grants, extensions, signatures, installed software)"
  :readme/note
  "Read-only inventory of what a Mac runs and what it may do: launchd jobs, login
   items, configuration profiles, kernel and system extensions, Chromium-family
   browser extensions with risky-permission analysis, TCC privacy grants from BOTH
   databases, code-signature verdicts, listening sockets resolved to real
   executable paths, installed applications with versions, and Gatekeeper/XProtect
   state.

   Two invariants: (1) no probe may raise an authorisation or consent dialog —
   sfltool dumpbtm requests system.privilege.admin and is deliberately never
   called; (2) a probe that cannot see reports :denied/:partial with a reason,
   never an empty result that reads as a clean machine. combine-status degrades
   rather than overwrites so a denial cannot be masked by a success elsewhere.

   macos-inventory.core is pure and holds every parser, tested against verbatim
   captured command output — lsof/profiles/systemextensionsctl/kmutil all emit
   shapes that are easy to almost-parse, and codesign writes its report to stderr
   while exiting 0 (reading only stdout made every signature verdict :unknown).
   macos-inventory.host (nbb) runs the probes.

   Reports enough to DETECT outdated apps (version, _MASReceipt, SUFeedURL) but
   never installs anything. Distinct from kotoba-lang/com-macos-darwin, which is a
   clean-room protocol-compat actor. Grown out of gftdcojp/ai-gftd-misogi
   (ADR-260726, com-junkawasaki/root)."}]
